From: ua5e83218c834e013 at groups.google.com (Michael Betancourt)
Date: Sun, 02 Oct 2016 01:52:46 +0000
Subject: Re: [stan-users] Ideas for Hierarchical Modeling With Partially Ordered Groups?
Message-ID: <zV5Qxu06FiM.0wwaf91-BgAJ@stan-users.groups.google.com>
In-Reply-To: <zV5Qxu06FiM.b6YvBgcOBgAJ@stan-users.groups.google.com>
References: <zV5Qxu06FiM.519nXsC9BQAJ@stan-users.groups.google.com> <zV5Qxu06FiM.b6YvBgcOBgAJ@stan-users.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/stan-users/c/zV5Qxu06FiM/m/0wwaf91-BgAJ>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

The big aspect that is lost when imposing exchangeability on ordered
groups is the sense of continuity.  Continuity implies that neighboring
groups should have similar values but exchangeability ignores that
implication.  Now if the groups are big enough then this constraint is
weak and the exchangeability assumption is quite good (for example,
with large age or income brackets) but if the groups are small then
this can be a serious issue.  A common example of the latter is when
the groups correspond to small intervals of time.

