You didn't say what the error was.  It helps to get the full
output.  Then it'll tell you if there's an undefined variable,
which would indicate an ordering problem or if some of the types
aren't right (usually need to convert elementwise operations
to regular ones or vice-versa or declare arrays as vectors/matrices).

- Bob

> On Oct 4, 2016, at 5:28 PM, ralphe...@gmail.com wrote:
>
> 215 is the sampling statement line
> to_vector(data_matrix) ~ normal(to_vector(mu), sig2obs);
>
> After playing around, this syntax seems to work (simplified to baseline only below)
> functions {
>   ...
> }
>
> data {
> ...
> }
>
> parameters{
>     //Background
>     vector[Nx]                  baseline_At_raw;
>     vector[Nx]                  baseline_Rt_raw;
>     vector[Nx]                  baseline_Ot_raw;
>
>     real<lower=0>               baseline_sig2At;
>     real<lower=0>               baseline_sig2Rt;
>     real<lower=0>               baseline_sig2Ot;
>
>     //Observation Noise
>     real<lower=0>               sig2obs;
> }
>
>
> transformed parameters{
>     vector[Nx]                  baseline_At;
>     vector[Nx]                  baseline_Rt;
>     vector[Nx]                  baseline_Ot;
>
>     //Baseline Priors
>     baseline_At[1] =  50 + baseline_At_raw[1]*baseline_sig2At;
>     baseline_Rt[1] = .01 + baseline_Rt_raw[1]*baseline_sig2Rt;
>     baseline_Ot[1] = -25 + baseline_Ot_raw[1]*baseline_sig2Ot;
>
>     for (x in 2:Nx){
>             baseline_At[x] = baseline_At[x-1] + baseline_At_raw[x]*baseline_sig2At;
>             baseline_Rt[x] = baseline_Rt[x-1] + baseline_Rt_raw[x]*baseline_sig2Rt;
>             baseline_Ot[x] = baseline_Ot[x-1] + baseline_Ot_raw[x]*baseline_sig2Ot;
>     }
> }
>
> model {
>     //Model variables
>     matrix[Nx, Ny] mu; //Holds the mean
>
>     //Set up priors for the sigmas
>     sig2obs ~ cauchy(0,.5);
>
>     bg_sig2At ~ cauchy(0, 1);
>     bg_sig2Rt ~ cauchy(0,.01);
>     bg_sig2Ot ~ cauchy(0, 1);
>
>     //Keep estimated parameters on the same scale
>     bg_Ot_raw[1:Nx] ~ normal(0,1);
>     bg_Rt_raw[1:Nx] ~ normal(0,1);
>     bg_At_raw[1:NX] ~ normal(0,1);
>
>     for (x in 1:Nx){
>         for (y in 1:Ny){
>             //Add Background
>             mu[x,y] = (baseline_At[x])*(1-baseline_Rt[x])^yvals[y]+baseline_Ot[x];
>         }
>     }
>
>       to_vector(data_matrix) ~ normal(to_vector(mu), sig2obs);
> }
>
> but fails when I convert the loop with lines such as
> baseline_At[x] = baseline_At[x-1] + baseline_At_raw[x]*baseline_sig2At;
>
> into the vectorized form
> baseline_At[2:Nx] = baseline_At[1:(Nx-1)] + baseline_At_raw[2:Nx]*baseline_sig2At;
> or if add more peak parameters. Could this have to do with the order of the statements, in that the distributions for the sigmas and the raws are declared after the variable transform? It will not let me place those statements in the main body of the model.
