Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Stan programs must have support for every value of
parameters that satisfy the declared constraints.  At that
point, you can usually get away with random inits and won't
see these reports.

You can debug with print() statements to figure out where
your log density is going to -infinity.

- Bob

> On Oct 10, 2016, at 11:16 AM, Tomoki <t.matsu...@gmail.com> wrote:
>
> Hi Daniel. Thank you for answering my question.
>
> I was confused what K and S express (since in this case, K = S).
>
> I've changed my R & RStan codes:
>
> R code
> library(rstan)
> library(bayesm)
> library(dplyr)
> rstan_options(auto_write = TRUE)
> options(mc.cores = parallel::detectCores())
>
> data("customerSat")
>
> customerSat <- customerSat[1:100,]
>
> # Figure CS3.8
> sat_max <- apply(customerSat, 1 , max)
> sat_min <- apply(customerSat, 1 , min)
> sat_range <- sat_max - sat_min + runif(length(sat_max), -0.3, 0.3)
> sat_med <- apply(customerSat, 1 , median) + runif(length(sat_max), -0.3, 0.3)
>
> plot(sat_med, sat_range)
>
>
> # Table CS3.1
> Table3.1 <- data.frame(Q = seq(1,10), Mean = as.vector(apply(customerSat, 2, mean))) %>%
>   bind_cols(as.data.frame(cov(customerSat)))
>
>
> # implementation Stan
> d.dat <- list(N=nrow(customerSat), S=ncol(customerSat), K=10,
>               X=customerSat,
>               nu_lambda=20, Lambda_bar=matrix(c(4, 0, 0, 0.5), 2, 2))
> d.model <- stan_model("case_study_3.stan")
> d.fit <- sampling(d.model, data=d.dat, iter=1000, chains=1)
>
>
>
> Stan code
> functions{
>   matrix delete_row_col(matrix a, int n) {
>     int N;
>     matrix[rows(a) - 1, cols(a) - 1] result;
>     N = rows(a);
>     for (i in 1:(n-1)) {
>       for (j in 1:(n-1))
>         result[i,j] = a[i,j];
>       for (j in (n+1):N)
>         result[i,j-1] = a[i,j];
>     }
>     for (i in (n+1):N) {
>       for (j in 1:(n-1))
>         result[i-1,j] = a[i,j];
>       for (j in (n+1):N)
>         result[i-1,j-1] = a[i,j];
>     }
>     return result;
>   }
>   vector delete_row(vector a, int n) {
>     int N;
>     int k;
>     vector[rows(a) - 1] result;
>     N = rows(a);
>     k = 1;
>     for (i in 1:N){
>       if(i != n){
>         result[k] = a[i];
>         k = k + 1;
>       }
>     }
>     return result;
>   }
> }
> data{
>   // # of observation
