2.  That's true in high dimensions---that was a slide from
the NUTS paper of Hoffman and Gelman (as cited on the slide!)

3.  ShinyStan's using our algorithms, so that's great.

4.  If two systems fit the same model, then they have
the same predictive accuracy by definition.  If not, then
one of them didn't do the fit properly.

- Bob

> On Oct 19, 2016, at 3:22 PM, krishna mohan <krish4...@gmail.com> wrote:
>
> Thanks Bob for getting back.
>
> 1. As I learn more about Stan ( and RStan & CmdStan) I'm seeing that how I set up RStan for comparison will be critical.
> 2. I finally got to read your presentation slides (the Vanderbilt slides) that compares MH, Gibbs, HMC with the NUT sampler and I see that Stan with NUT sampler is a lot more efficient than the MH and Gibbs. Other forum members too have advised me that my 20,000 iterations is an overkill.
> 3. For posterior analysis on Stan output I have been using ShinyStan. Are there any others that you would recommend?
> 4. In the comparison results I will include the specifications used, time to complete the analyses and the predictive accuracy too.
>
> My motivation to learn Stan and RStan as an extension has been that I will be able to use Stan in everyday modelling exercises and also with more complex data sets, I definitely feel I'm getting there. Thanks again for your feedback and advice
