Hi Corey

Could you estimate the weights as parameters in addition to the model parameters using RStan or rstanarm? I also work with survey data a lot and the application of weights, I often do after developing the (predictive) models.

If you have weights, say by an age or an ethnicity variable, those weights could also be additionally estimated (makes the model hierarchical in some ways as your weights will differ by a grouping variable). You could set your prior to be between 0,1 and the sum of weights (/priors) to be equal to 1.

Let me know how it works out. I will see if I can set up example model with and without weights for starters.

Krishna

On Saturday, May 21, 2016 at 2:39:59 PM UTC-4, CoreySparks wrote:
> Regarding the "generating model" idea, Snijders and Bosker spend a lot of time comparing the "model based" regression coefficient estimates, which are derived from the hierarchical model with the level 2 units included to model the between-stratum variance,  to the "design based" model coefficients which are derived from a model which weights the likelihood function.  They present a pretty thorough exploratory treatment of influential units (in terms of their weights). From my reading there is not an agreed up standard, and if all else fails, the model based estimates correspond better with the Bayesian framework of modeling. IMO
