From u8aa9b8cc06fdc99c at groups.google.com  Fri Oct 21 18:47:31 2016
From: u8aa9b8cc06fdc99c at groups.google.com (Bob Carpenter)
Date: Fri, 21 Oct 2016 18:47:31 +0000
Subject: Re: [stan-users] Rstan gaussian mixture model 'Scale parameter is 0, but must be > 0'
Message-ID: <oRjipiXp_gA.CrJlZMA7BwAJ@stan-users.groups.google.com>
In-Reply-To: <oRjipiXp_gA.DFOv4kokBwAJ@stan-users.groups.google.com>
References: <oRjipiXp_gA.pa1cfWIjBwAJ@stan-users.groups.google.com> <oRjipiXp_gA.DFOv4kokBwAJ@stan-users.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/stan-users/c/oRjipiXp_gA/m/CrJlZMA7BwAJ>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

sigma is occassionally hitting 0 due to underflow in the transforms.

There's a problem with mixture models in general that they don't have
maximum likelihoods.  If you put one mixture component be responsible
for one data item, as the scale (std dev param) goes to zero and the location
(mean param) goes to the data item's value, the likelihood grows.

One way to avoid this is to force the components to have more responsibility.
You can do this with a prior on the simplex.  Something like Dirichlet(alpha)
where alpha is a vector values >> 1 can help.  Also putting a prior on the
scales that explicitly avoids zero (like a lognormal) can help.

- Bob

