At the bottom of the wiki entry on prior choice recommendations (https://github.com/stan-dev/stan/wiki/Prior-Choice-Recommendations) is a section for Gaussian Processes. The first bullet describes the student_t(4,0,1) prior for inv_rho (called "rho" in the code Bistoon linked because I was being a lazy typist). The second bullet refers to the eta & sigma parameters, and that setting the same prior for each is akin to setting a prior on the signal-to-noise as peaked at .5, but that bullet refers to a 0 peaked normal(0,1), which I've subsequently been replacing with weibul(2,1), which is peaked at a little less than 1 and goes to zero at 0.

--
Mike Lawrence
Graduate Student
Department of Psychology & Neuroscience
Dalhousie University

~ Certainty is (possibly) folly ~

