Subject: Discrete Variable Conditional on Continuous Variable: How to Fit in Stan? (Exponential Distribution)
Message-ID: <N_bvrXFwX3M.dF89LhRvAwAJ@stan-users.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/stan-users/c/N_bvrXFwX3M/m/dF89LhRvAwAJ>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

I'm trying to wrap my head around a toy example for a much harder problem. I'm interested in modelling the probability of detection of some event conditional on the arrival time of that event. As an example I'm using the exponential distribution. Let the arrival time of the i^th event be distributed as exponential with some rate parameter, lambda. If the arrival time is less than 1, the event is detected with probability p_1; and if the arrival time is greater than 1, the event is detect with with probability p_2. "Detection" is probably the wrong word here though. Because I am assuming that if an event is not detected, we know that it occurred, but we don't the arrival time. In other words, given that arrival time is less than 1 we record a zero with probability (1-p_1) and if arrival time is greater than 1 we record a zero with probability (1-p_2).

This is easily simulated in R as follows:

y = rexp(1000)
set.seed(36)
p = plogis(rnorm(2))

x = rep(NA, 1000)
z = rep(NA, 1000)

for (i in 1:1000){
  x[i] = rbinom(1,1, p[(y[i] > 1) + 1])
  z[i] = x[i]*y[i]
}

As a proof of concept I can fit this model in JAGS as follows:

library(rjags)

jags_proof_of_concept <-"
model{
  for (i in 1:N) {
      y[i] ~ dexp(rate)
      x[i] ~ dbern(ifelse(y[i] <= 1, p[1], p[2]))
  }
  p[1] ~ dbeta(1,1)
  p[2] ~ dbeta(1,1)
  rate ~ dgamma(.01,.01)
}"

jags_POC = jags.model(textConnection(jags_proof_of_concept),
                  data = list(N = length(x),
                              x = x,
                              y = ifelse(x == 0, NA, y)),
                  n.chains = 4,
                  n.adapt  = 1000
                  )

jags_out <- coda.samples(model=jags_POC,
            variable.names=c("rate", "p"),
            n.iter=1000, thin=1)
summary(jags_out)

Now in Stan, I know that I need to marginalize out the discrete parameters. I've attempted to do so, but I'm clearly missing something, miscoded something, or am barking up the wrong MCMC tree.

I have that: P(X > x | x > 0) = p_1* (lambda * exp(-lambda*x)) I(0 < x <1) +  p_2* (lambda * exp(-lambda*x)) I(1 < x)

where I() is the indicator function.

And P(X = 0) = (1- p_1) * (1 - e^(-lamba)) + (1 - p_2) * e^(-lambda), where 1- e^-lambda is simply the CDF of the exponential distribution evaluated at 1 ( Pr(y <1) ) and e^-lamba is the inverse CDF evaluated at 1 ( Pr( y > 1).

I coded this up as follows:

stan_proof_of_concept <-"
data {
  int<lower = 0> N_obs;
  int<lower = 0> N_mis;
  real<lower =0> y_obs[N_obs];
}

parameters {
  real<lower = 0> rate;
  real<lower = 0, upper = 1> p[2];
}

model {

  for(i in 1:N_mis)
    target += log_sum_exp(log1m(p[1]) + exponential_lcdf(1|rate),
                          log1m(p[2]) + exponential_lccdf(1|rate));

  for(i in 1:N_obs)
    target += log_sum_exp(log(p[1]) + (y_obs[i] <= 1) + exponential_lpdf(y_obs[i]|rate),
                            log(p[2]) + (y_obs[i] > 1) + exponential_lpdf(y_obs[i]|rate));
}
"

dat = list(N_obs = sum(z != 0),
           N_mis = sum(z == 0),
           y_obs = z[z != 0])

stan_POC <- stan(model_code = stan_proof_of_concept, data = dat, pars = c("rate","p"))

The model runs, but I am not able to successfully retrieve the simulated values. Am I simply asking for something with discrete parameters that Stan can't do? Did I screw up up with marginalizing? Coding problem? In short, what am I missing here?

This is a proof of concept for a much harder problem with tens of thousands of observations. JAGS takes far too long with this dataset.

